me`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1552:`register_test`; semantic corpus streams/ffi/park with scheduled events. Add\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1633:all named register tests and every semantic golden through the harness; report\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1673:continuations or async-stack capture, and no full semantic VM feature parity\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1713:register fixture schema or restore semantics.\ndocs/design/dao.await.md:366:- VM-specific load-program functions in the AST walker, stack VM, register VM,\ndocs/design/dao.await.md:367:  and semantic VM.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:81:linker for the semantic VM and a third for the walker. There is one\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:217:> semantic VM's canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2). **This\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:788:### 5.2 `:yin.semantic/code` (the semantic VM)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:791:> stack-shaped v3 semantic VM and is historical: its validator namespace\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:862:### 5.4 `:yin.debruijn.register` (the register VM)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1484:     The register VM's call frame today carries the caller's whole\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:2443:       formats -- executes under the semantic VM with results equal\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:6:2026-09-15: the map AST is the Universal AST's canonical representation that gets evaluated by the ast-walker (a LISP using maps, not a LISP using vectors instead of lists). However, the Universal AST is converted into flat per-node tuple rows (`[id tag & slots]`) that act like bytecode, preserving everything in the Universal AST so that the semantic VM can execute it quickly because of the linearization of the AST into tuples (the VM lowers these rows into an instruction vector for execution). These rows are what is content-hashed, stored, streamed, and named — there is no nested tuple tree (ruling 1 of 2026-09-14,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:147:  load — the walker reconstructs the map AST from rows, the semantic VM\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:169:The fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the `:lambda` arm already binds `params` by name, and the `:variable` arm already resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (`ast_walker.cljc:361-363`; the semantic VM's `:var` opcode does the same at `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this design keeps that fallthrough rather than splitting free and bound references into separate node types (§4.5). Separately, closure application (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`; `semantic.cljc:200`) still binds arguments with `(zipmap params args)`, which leaves missing parameter names unbound rather than bound to `nil` — that binding change belongs to the application arm and the semantic VM's apply, not to `:lambda`.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:488:semantic VM has no opcode for it, and no test constructs one (a search of\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:528:`:stream-close` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:12-16`), the semantic VM\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1260:AST it reconstructed at load; the semantic VM runs `(aget code pc)` over\ndocs/design/yang.typescript.md:120:  register VMs; the AST walker under the linked profile waits on L-b, exactly\ndocs/design/yang.typescript.md:1431:|       | parserless on Node and Dart      | error, no body side effect,       |\ndocs/design/yang.typescript.md:1657:|                             | parserless (rows)                              |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:672:**Amended 2026-10-16 (any-pc, owner invariant 2026-10-17).** The register-shaped machine has no unserializable interior: its task state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩` with its ordered waits and completed wakes, is canonical (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §4.1; continuation design §1.3). **A continuation is representable at every instruction boundary**; the lift refuses a value with no wire form, a dependency the receiver cannot satisfy, or an observation in flight, never a pc. A *safepoint* is a boundary at which the program stops by itself: a park with a pending wait, an explicit park with none, or a halt with a result. The paragraph above describes the stack-shaped machine, which left at phase 8. The first draft's table\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1199:above was written for the named semantic VM alone. The linker's four\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
