{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '125,160p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; sed -n '1760,1820p' src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc; sed -n '1,30p' test/yang/python/antlr/oracle_parity_test.cljc; sed -n '1,100p' test/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe_test.cljc 2>/dev/null || true; sed -n '1,60p' test/yang/python/antlr/repl_probe_test.clj 2>/dev/null || true\"","aggregated_output":"   it is asked, so without a bound a program that calls `q` without end\n   would hold its input round in the drive forever.  Each call re-reads\n   the published index, so a thousand calls is already a long round; a\n   program past the bound is stopped with `query-call-limit-text` as its\n   error and its round rolls back like any failed round.\"\n  1024)\n\n\n(def query-call-limit-text\n  (str \"the evaluation called dao.space.query/q more than \"\n       query-drive-budget\n       \" times and was stopped (:yin.repl.query/call-limit)\"))\n\n\n(def vm-constructors\n  \"The evaluators on `yin.vm`: the ast-walker, the semantic register VM,\n   and the nameless de Bruijn stack and register kernels, each starting with an empty image.\"\n  {:ast-walker ast-walker/create-vm\n   :semantic semantic/create-vm\n   :stack #(stack/create-vm [] %)\n   :register #(register/create-vm nil %)})\n\n\n(def vm-labels\n  {:ast-walker \"ASTWalkerVM\"\n   :semantic \"SemanticVM\"\n   :stack \"DebruijnStackVM\"\n   :register \"DebruijnRegisterVM\"})\n\n\n(defn- packet->vector\n  [packet]\n  (:vector (linearize/project-rows (macro/packet->row-set packet))))\n\n\n(defn- append-stack-image\n                 (:render binding) (assoc :render (:render binding)))]\n    (if-let [lower-session (:lower-session binding)]\n      (let [[ast session] (lower-session parsed (:frontend-session state))]\n        (vary-meta ast assoc ::frontend (assoc staged :session session)))\n      (cond-> ((:lower binding) parsed)\n        (seq staged) (vary-meta assoc ::frontend staged)))))\n\n\n(defn- compile-input\n  \"Lower `input` through the selected frontend; `forms` are the shell\n   reader's forms, used by a frontend that consumes them.\"\n  [state input forms]\n  (let [binding (frontend-binding state (:lang state))]\n    (lower-parsed state binding\n                  ((:parse binding)\n                   (if (= :forms (:input binding)) forms input)))))\n\n\n(defn- forms-input?\n  [state]\n  (= :forms (:input (frontend-binding state (:lang state)))))\n\n\n(defn- compile-source\n  [state input]\n  (let [binding (frontend-binding state (:lang state))]\n    (lower-parsed state binding\n                  ((:parse binding)\n                   (if (= :forms (:input binding)) (read-forms input) input)))))\n\n\n(defn- compile-command-ast\n  \"The AST `(compile ...)` previews.  It lowers against a primed frontend\n   session: a session-staged frontend carries its prelude only on the\n   session's first lowering, and the preview is of the submitted form, not\n   of the runtime it would run on.  The session itself is not touched.\"\n  [state arg]\n  (let [state (assoc state :frontend-session :primed)]\n    (cond\n      (string? arg) (compile-source state arg)\n      (forms-input? state) (compile-input state nil [arg])\n      :else (throw (ex-info \"This compile command expects a source string\"\n                            {:lang (:lang state) :arg arg})))))\n\n\n(defn- render-compile-output\n  \"Render the frontend syntax, the input row batch, and what the session's\n   expander would make of it against its current store: the expanded tree\n   packet (or the expansion error) and the event rows.  The expansion is a\n   preview; its context is discarded, so nothing is committed.\"\n  [state ast]\n  (let [batch (encoder/program-batch ast)\n        {:keys [tree error log]}\n        (macro/expand-batch batch (get-in state [:expander :ctx]))]\n    (str \"AST:\\n\" (format-value ast)\n         \"\\n\\nInput rows:\\n\" (format-value batch)\n         (if tree\n           (str \"\\n\\nExpanded rows:\\n\" (format-value tree))\n           (str \"\\n\\nExpansion error:\\n\" (format-value error)))\n         \"\\n\\nEvents:\\n\" (format-value (second log)))))\n\n(ns yang.python.antlr.oracle-parity-test\n  \"J2b(a) (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.8): this host's\n   Python parser against the committed JVM oracle for the fixed jolt corpus\n   (test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_*.edn), so the Node, Dart and\n   jolt parsers are all judged against one oracle. On the JVM it is a\n   regeneration check: the oracle is what the JVM produces now.\n\n   Per entry: strict packet equality is recorded (a RECORD line with the\n   packet's dao.jing content address, the byte-level identity of its\n   canonical encoding); the asserted contract is E1, the equivalence the\n   jolt adapter meets (src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj): an ok packet is equal\n   in full; an error packet is equal apart from :yang.cst/errors, has at\n   least one error, and its first error's :kind, :span, :line and :column\n   equal the JVM's, with a non-empty :message (message text, expecting sets\n   and later errors are exempt). cst/first-failure and the REPL\n   completeness probe must answer as on the JVM; the probe's\n   first-failure call sequence is compared where vars can be redefined\n   (not ClojureDart). The input-policy corpus also checks that the host read\n   each source to the JVM's code points.\n\n   J2b(b), the restricted lowering comparison: for every entry whose packet\n   is ok on this host and in the oracle, the shared lowering\n   (yang.python.antlr.lower/lower-module-body) of this host's packet is\n   recorded by its dao.jing content address, or its refusal. The corpus\n   holds no float and no integer beyond 64 bits (W8), so a numeric host\n   difference cannot masquerade as a parser one. Cross-host equality is read\n   from the RECORD lines of each lane, the JVM's being the reference; here\n   the host's lowering of its own packet must equal its lowering of the\n   oracle packet.\"\n  (:require\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
